you were joined by the schools minister  nick gibb atmoscape lovely to have you  on the show this morning  um plenty of course to talk about on  your brief and we we will move to that  but i wonder if we can for just first  start with  the state of the brexit negotiations i  suppose it it depends on who you listen  to  and indeed who you believe or on the one  hand ursula wonderland  seem to be speaking quite positively  boris johnson now suggesting once again  that perhaps no deal the likeliest  outcome what's the truth of it all well  it is a very serious  situation we will test every route to  seeking a free trade agreement with the  european union  but we cannot do so at the expense of  our national sovereignty you know we  can't be the only nation on earth  uh that doesn't have control of its own  waters its own borders  we made a very clear commitment during  the referendum in the manifesto that we  would take back control of our  money our laws and our borders and that  does include the sea  and it does include making sure that in  any sort of uh  level playing field arrangement that we  we have control of our laws and those  are the red lines that the government is  negotiating we want a free trade deal  but the eu will have to move uh if we  are going to secure that deal  but just in terms of the sticking points  and you know with the greatest  of respect to those who take to the sea  to catch fish  and the british fishing industry  contributes you know less than one  percent to our  of our of our gdp is it worth  the financial impact that not getting a  trade deal will have  to essentially you know stand up for a  point of principle about  access to our waters they're not asking  for it in perpetuity  it does appear that they're asking for  just a few too many years  well it is important for an independent  sovereign nation  to have control of its its seas around  around the country that we would be the  only country in the world that didn't  but we are prepared we will do  everything we can to get a free trade  deal we'll test every route but we are  prepared  for a for no free trade deal uh  arrangements you know  liz trust has been negotiating free  trade deals around the world with  japan with canada mutual recognition  agreements united states australia new  zealand free trade agreements with  countries in  south america south korea singapore and  so on  and we are very well prepared uh we've  been preparing uh for for many many  months for  uh for life outside of a free trade  arrangement or outside  life outside the european union uh with  extra staff in hmrc  and so on and plans in place for the  board for that for our ports and so on  so  we're prepared for both eventualities  but we we are doing everything we can  to seek us a free trade agreement with  the european union of course the  the other item in the prime minister's  inbox at the moment is covert 19  and christmas um yesterday of course we  had the announcement  about various areas moving into tier  three you know come the weekend  almost 70 percent of england's  population is going to be in tier three  the tier system well at the very least  tier two  doesn't seem to work well the theater  the tier system  is a good system it means you can focus  your restrictions on areas of the  country with the highest rates of  infection we have seen  success in the northwest that's been in  tier three for weeks  we're seeing their infection rates  falling very rapidly and and we'll move  people up  tears and down tears we've moved down  tears and it seems  with a few exceptions we seem to be  moving in one direction  and that is to the highest level of to  the highest tier  on that basis you know you've got places  like greater manchester complaining well  they thought that they should come down  you know i say again with 70 with 70  of the england's population now in the  top tier how on earth is tier 2 worked  well we're seeing very serious rise in  cases 28 percent increase and just in  the last week 14  more emissions to hospitals because of  people now being in each other's company  because of the relaxation of the people  well we have to we have to  we will take action whenever uh there  are risers in infections and that's what  the tier system does and that's why matt  hancock yesterday announced further  counties going into tier three but if  you look at the r  rate we're now between point nine and  one across the uk  point between point eight and one in  england uh and if you think back to the  beginning of the pandemic when it was  over three we have achieved a huge  amount but and we are very close with  one of the first countries we are the  first country in the world to be rolling  out a vaccine 138 000 people have been  vaccinated we are very close  but we're not there yet and we cannot uh  take our foot off the accelerator in  in terms of tackling this virus and the  government will do whatever it takes  to tackle this deadly uh and virulent  uh virus that figure you mentioned there  138 000 in terms of vaccinations  that's the figure we got from the the  nadine zahavi the other day isn't it do  we  we don't yet have an updated total well  that's a that's the latest figure that's  been published so that's the thing that  i can use today  can we then talk about the testing  scheme  as regards your particular brief schools  everyone understands  that mass testing in schools will be a  key element  of us getting ourselves out of this uh  pandemic  how exactly is this going to work well  we're offering tests to  half five and a half million uh  secondary school pupils  it's about breaking the chain of  transmission following the christmas  period  so from the fourth of january um all  children will go back into primary  school  those children who are in an exam year  will start school in the 4th of january  but for the other secondary school  pupils  we will they will be learning from home  through remote education for those first  five days while schools get ready  to implement this testing regime to test  five and a half million people so  twice who will be administering the test  system within schools there was a  suggestion for a while that it was going  to be the armed forces it appears this  morning  actually teachers are going to have to  do it well the armed forces will help  with some of the planning  but the administration of the test will  be volunteers and  agency workers and there'll be some  funding for schools to help pay for  those agency workers  uh it's not about volunteers who on  earth is going to volunteer to go into  the petri dish that is our education  system at the moment and  and carry out testing well there's ppe  for available and also you know people  are very community minded this is a  national effort  to make sure that we are testing five  and a half million people so we have  more children  this is about our priority for education  making sure more children are in the  classroom  getting their lessons continuing their  education  so that in the spring and the summer we  have more students in school  rather than self-isolating when there is  a test for every positive test in a  school  on average about 24 students have to  self-isolate this is to avoid that we  want more children  in class learning continuing their  education but believe you me i am very  well of their children being  being forced to self-isolate at the  moment but but but i say it again  you have a situation in which clearly  mass testing in schools is going to be  of great importance in defeating the  pandemic  teachers get told about it on the last  full day of term the last full teaching  day of time  they are complaining that they simply do  not have the time nor the resources to  get this set up  by the time that people's return well we  announced it yesterday the 17th of  december it takes  place from the 4th of january next year  we've been piloting these tests in  schools for a number of weeks  and also on tuesday we announced that we  will be testing every  member of staff in secondary schools  once a week  and any uh any person that has a close  contact of somebody  who's tested positive in a secondary  school including the pupils will be  tested every day for seven days  and if they test negative they can  remain in school rather than  self-isolating so all these plans have  been in place but we are  acting rapidly in response to increasing  infection rates and that's the right  thing for the government to be doing  can i just speak with you absolutely  certain are teachers going to be  asked to have any role in the testing of  their pupils  no that their role is to make sure that  we continue  teaching pupils in school and also the  remote education for those first five  days from the fourth of january for  secondary school people  because the national education union  chief says that you're sending out a  video to schools a half hour video that  teachers are having to watch  and that they would be involved in this  process i just want to be absolutely  clear  teachers will have no role in this be it  you know swabbing  or carrying out the test or carrying out  the administration of the test yes  teachers are already fully occupied  in keeping the school secure in behavior  of pupils and teaching them in catch-up  and so on and remote education  they already have their hands full in  terms of the education side of what's  happening in our schools  look you know the the school system the  teachers head teachers in this country  have been magnificent during this  pandemic  uh keeping schools open helping you know  keeping those schools secure  and i take my hat off as we all do to  the work that they have been doing and  they will and i'm sure they'll respond  extremely well  to this next phase of mass testing in in  january  of course in the past few days we have  seen your boss education secretary and  and greenwich council  you know at loggerheads using  essentially the threat of legal action  to get the schools to reopen  was that entirely advisable given that  we are now instructing schools  to start making plans for not just  distance  learning and online learning but a  staggered return to school in the new  year  i mean the political capital you may  have used up with teachers  was it was it worthwhile expanding that  yes it's important  you know education is a national  priority we wanted schools we want  schools to  remain open right through to uh to the  end of term  in fact schools have delivered that 99  of schools have been open  uh since september uh with very high  attendance rates  about 85 percent attendance rates across  the system so it's not for a local  authority  to take a decision to close a school  what's happening in january  is a national decision to roll out the  mass testing of  five and a half million secondary school  students and that's why we're closed  you know why for those first five days  uh half of about half a second to school  pupils will be  having their lessons remotely at home  but then on the 11th of january they  will all  go back into school and schools will be  open primary schools secondary schools  all will be open from the fourth of  january  the head of the royal college of nursing  and dem donna canair has said that that  the relaxation at christmas  could lead to a tsunami as she describes  it  of of covert 19 cases can you just  comment on  what we've seen in northern ireland they  are going into full lockdown essentially  on boxing day  we hear reports in the times the mail  this morning about discussions about  tier four  or a national lockdown uh for the whole  of england  that's where we're heading isn't it well  nothing is ruled out of course as we  tackle this pandemic but the tier system  is a very effective way  of using test data that identifies where  the local  uh spikes in infection rates are and to  focus the restrictions on those areas so  you go into tier three if the  the infection rates are rising in  bristol and north somerset and  herefordshire we've seen  areas reduce come down the tier system  as the infection rates in those areas  are  are are coming down it is a way of  focusing the restriction and it is a  very effective system it's worked in the  north west where we've seen  of tier three area the infection rates  coming down but given what we've seen in  northern and i think we have to be  absolutely clear about this the  government is  not ruling out post-christmas a national  lockdown of the type we saw you know in  the first in the  first lockdown with the closing of  non-essential shops  restrictions or not ban on commuting  these are things that are being  discussed in government well that's not  what i said we have a very effective  uh tear system we think that the locally  focused restrictions  is a very effective way of tackling the  virus but we will do everything we can  to tackle the virus we're so close now  we've got  a vaccine 138 000 people as we discussed  uh having a vaccine with one of the  first country in the world to do that  but we cannot take the foot off the uh  the accelerator until we have tackled  this pandemic until  everybody is vaccinated that's why it's  a very good news story that we're able  to  deliver 11 million of these lateral flow  test devices to schools so five and a  half million secondary school pupils  can be tested so we can have more young  people in the classroom learning every  day in the spring term  and in the summertime leading up to  their exams in the end and the end of  the school year

